// the problem is to find a subset with all same elements
// of maximum size such that their sum is not formable by
// taking any combination of the other elements other than
// those in the subset (the 'other' could include 'that'
// 'same' element itself too)

// so dp state is dp[sum][k elements] where 'k' is the number
// of elements used to form the 'sum'

// eg: (5 5 5), (4 5 6) and hence dp[15][3] = 2 ways

// now you can't iterate over elements of the array like
// in knapsack/subset sum problem as in the case of duplicates
// you'll end up counting the same way twice.

// Eg: if arr = 5 5 5
// knapsack ind 1:
// dp[5][1] = 1 dp[10][2] = 0
// knapsack ind 2:
// dp[5][1] = 2 dp[10][2] = 1
// knapsack ind 3:
// dp[5][1] = 3 dp[10][2] = 3
// which is not what we want for dp[10][2]
// if sum is composed of same elements, we want it to be
// counted as just 1 way.

// 20
// 50 50 50 50 39 39 9 9 24 24 96 90 90 77 59 98 98 77 96 80
// that happens for 150 = 50*3 in the above case
// hence the first method in main below doesn't work
// i.e. commented out code

// however, if you change the update to only include
// values from the previous distinct value, then it works
// i.e.: 4 4 5 5
// for 4,4 do one pass for all relevant (sum,k) for dp[sum][k]
// for 5,5 do the next pass, but use old dp[sum][k] from previous 4,4
// 	pass to update values for this 5,5 combo
// this is illustrated in the accepted code

// way better than this using two dp arrays with last index
// indicating which is old and which is new, is to fill in some
// order so that what we want holds - this is done by Swistakk's
// solution below, he fills it in descending order for (sum,k)
// in that way we don't repeat values.

