// https://codeforces.com/contest/1271/problem/E

/*

Mistake -
1 : The approach that if we cannot make cnt >= k using mid ; then we can never make cnt >= k using any mid greater
than mid is wrong

Reason :
Let say mid = 3 ; and maxx = 11
we can make only cnt = 3 using current mid ( 3 , 6 , 7 ).

But for a greater mid - mid = 4 ; we are allowed to use the odd term after it ( 5 ) too ; and we can unfold its
recursion tree too.
Hence using that ; f(4) = 6.

So at this point - there is a conflict.
Should we reject the binary search or should we modify it.

This time ; this binary search can be modified.
Reason - the best candidate that gives us a greater f(mid) near to mid is mid + 1 ( in case mid is odd ).
This is because mid + 1 is even ; and thus we may use recursion tree of mid+2 also to increase our count !

SO we can check for mid-1 ; mid ; and mid+1.
if f(mid + delta) < k ; then we can say that we need to move left ; and do hi = mid - 1.
Else we may say we can move right and do lo = mid + 1.

*/

/*

Mistake 2 - TLE on test case 16.
Earlier ; I was trying to go on every node ; and I was doing f ( node*2 ) ; and f(2*node + 1) ;

But if we unfold the recursion tree ; we will see that it is a binary tree.
We can easily see that height of tree is logarithmic ; and for n <= 1e18 ; height <= 64.

But ; then what will be the number of nodes in the binary tree ?
It will be (1 << max_height) - 1 ( heights are taken 0 based ).

SO since max height can be upto 64 ; hence number of nodes >= 1e18 ( easily ). So we will get a TLE.

What can we do -
Observation : the nodes in the binary tree are consecutive at every level.
Hence ; if we keep a track of the level ; we will know the number of nodes in that level = (1 << level).

And we can see from the highest node will this entire block is <= maxx. If yes ; we may add 1 << level to answer.
Else ; we can find upto which index this holds true ; and we may add those many nodes !

Thus by maintaining only the max node of any level ; we will do only max_height number of iterations !
And thus we may solve function f() in log(1e18) time.

*/

